# Target C-II — exact pentagon lattice-phase regression

**Date:** 2026-09-12
**Classification:** regression / rejected bridge lemma
**LEVEL I STATUS:** **STILL OPEN. C-II REMAINS OPEN.**

This note gives an explicit family showing that a convex unit pentagonal contact face
can have all five edge directions arbitrarily close to the three projective lattice
lines at angles `0`, `pi/3`, and `2pi/3`, while every non-edge source separation is
strictly greater than one. Consequently, the hoped-for implication

```text
near-lattice phase purity + convex pentagonal face
    -> a transverse train endpoint with a uniform angular gap
```

is false without an additional quantitative nondegeneracy theorem.

## 1. The family

For `0<delta<delta_0`, put

\[
\alpha(\delta):=\arccos(\cos\delta-1/2)
\]

and take five successive unit edge vectors with arguments

\[
\theta_0=\frac\pi3-\alpha(\delta),\quad
\theta_1=\frac\pi3,\quad
\theta_2=\frac\pi3+\alpha(\delta),
\]

\[
\theta_3=\frac{4\pi}3-\delta,\quad
\theta_4=\frac{4\pi}3+\delta.
\tag{PR.1}
\]

Choose `delta_0` small enough that

\[
0<\alpha(\delta)<\frac{2\pi}3,
\qquad
\alpha(\delta)+\delta<\pi,
\qquad
2\delta<\frac{2\pi}3.
\tag{PR.2}
\]

The first three vectors sum to

\[
e^{i\pi/3}(1+2\cos\alpha)
=2\cos\delta\,e^{i\pi/3},
\]

whereas the final two sum to

\[
2\cos\delta\,e^{i4\pi/3}
=-2\cos\delta\,e^{i\pi/3}.
\]

Thus the five vectors close exactly. Their cyclic direction gaps are

\[
\alpha,\quad \alpha,\quad \pi-\alpha-\delta,
\quad 2\delta,\quad \pi-\alpha-\delta.
\tag{PR.3}
\]

By (PR.2), all gaps are positive and less than `2pi/3`. Therefore the closed polygon
is strictly convex.

## 2. All non-edge separations are strictly greater than one

For two consecutive edges whose direction gap is `g`, the corresponding two-edge
diagonal has length

\[
|e^{i\theta}+e^{i(\theta+g)}|=2\cos(g/2).
\]

Every gap in (PR.3) is strictly below `2pi/3`, so each such diagonal is strictly
greater than one. Every other diagonal of a pentagon is the negative of a consecutive
two-edge sum, because the five edge vectors close. Hence all five non-edge diagonals
are strictly greater than one.

The polygon is therefore compatible with unit separation and with the absence of an
additional unit contact chord. It is a legitimate strict convex pentagonal face at
the finite `delta` level.

## 3. Phase collapse

As `delta` tends to zero,

\[
\alpha(\delta)\longrightarrow\frac\pi3,
\]

and the projective edge directions in (PR.1) converge to the three lines

\[
[0],\quad [\pi/3],\quad [2\pi/3].
\]

The two final edges have arguments `4pi/3+-delta`, which project to a single one of
these three lines. The face remains strictly convex for every `delta>0`, and all
non-edge separations remain `>1`, although two of them tend to one as `delta->0`.

Thus a linearly large pentagon population can, at least locally, absorb endpoint
closure while its train directions approach the same three lattice phases that carry
the quadratic rhombus crossing density. No uniform transverse phase mass follows
from pentagon existence alone.

## 4. Frontier effect

This regression blocks the following proposed closure of the Section-146 endpoint:

1. phase saturation forces the atomic train directions into at most three lattice
   projective lines;
2. every pentagonal defect face must then use a transverse train direction;
3. the outer boundary has no capacity for those transverse endpoints.

Step 2 is false. The exact family above has no transverse projective direction in the
limit, and the strict finite source inequalities do not prevent that limit.

A valid closure must therefore supply one of the missing quantitative inputs:

- a uniform lower bound on the relevant pentagon diagonal slack;
- a source-minimality theorem that converts the near-unit diagonals into an admissible
  contact/descent operation;
- a metric coefficient bound on the pentagon closure rows; or
- a different coupling of the pentagon network to the diameter graph.

The construction is local and does not assert that these pentagons co-embed in a
global C-II minimizer. It is nevertheless an exact finite geometric regression to the
uniform face-phase bridge. Theorem-state authority is unchanged.
